{
  "dom_id": "step-q124154",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If the line $y=-2x-4$ and the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x+2}-1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-4 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x+2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-4 & =\\frac{1}{x+2}-1 \\\\ -2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\ (-2x-3)(x+2) & =1 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-7x-6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{(7)^{2}-4(2)(7)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{49-56}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{-7}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x-4$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{1}{x+2}-1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14267214/assets/593bf46d7d1f90a7c2c878c6ce3926fa10a7ab83814777ed1ec3830063bda694.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
